//? Title: Support Vector Machines: Margins, Kernels and Iris Classification
//? Subtitle: Hand-solvable examples, primal–dual checks and held-out predictions with datamunge's stats::SVM
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given labelled measurements, we want to classify a new observation even when the classes
cannot be separated perfectly by a straight line. The difficulty is to choose a boundary
that separates useful structure without following every training point. A support vector
machine balances the width of a separating margin against the cost of violating it.
We derive the soft-margin primal and dual, the kernel construction and the two-coordinate
SMO update. Four collinear points expose the support coefficients; two points give an exact
cost path; four corners show how a kernel separates XOR. A scale-and-cost sweep checks the
computed coefficients and objectives against closed forms. Finally, iris provides a three-class
example with training-only standardisation and a reproducible held-out evaluation: the
default RBF model correctly labels 44 of 45 held-out flowers. Pairwise
optimality residuals accompany the accuracy figures: reaching a sweep limit is not a proof
that the numerical optimiser has converged.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Separating labelled points with a margin

Start with observations \((x_i,y_i)\), where \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\) and
\(y_i\in\{-1,+1\}\). A hyperplane has decision value \(f(x)=w^\top x+b\).
Its perpendicular distance from \(x\) is \(|f(x)|/\lVert w\rVert\).
For separable data we can rescale \(w,b\) so the nearest points have
\(y_if(x_i)=1\). The planes \(f=+1\) and \(f=-1\) then have full separation
\(2/\lVert w\rVert\). Maximising this width gives

$$
\min_{w,b}\frac12\lVert w\rVert^2,
\qquad y_i(w^\top x_i+b)\geq1.
$$
\alttext(Minimise one half the squared norm of w, subject to each signed decision value being at least one.)

This fixes an arbitrary scale of the decision function; the sign alone would give the same
classifications for any positive rescaling. In the code, the alphabetically first of each
pair of classes is \(+1\); the second is \(-1\). A zero decision votes for the first.

>> Soft margins and hinge loss

Overlapping data cannot satisfy every hard constraint. Allow slack \(\xi_i\geq0\)
and penalise its sum with \(C>0\). At the optimum slack is the hinge loss:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P &= \min_{w,b,\xi}\left\{\frac12\lVert w\rVert^2+C\sum_i\xi_i\right\},
& y_if(x_i)&\geq1-\xi_i,\quad\xi_i\geq0,\\
\xi_i&=\max(0,1-y_if(x_i)),
& P(w,b)&=\frac12\lVert w\rVert^2+C\sum_i\max(0,1-y_if(x_i)).
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(The soft-margin primal minimises one half the squared norm plus C times the sum of slacks, subject to signed decisions at least one minus slack. The least feasible slack is the positive part of one minus the signed decision.)

A point with margin \(y_if(x_i)>1\) is outside the margin; one with margin between
0 and 1 is correctly classified but inside it; a negative margin is misclassified.
Smaller \(C\) makes violations cheaper. Larger \(C\) does not guarantee better predictions
on new observations. This implementation uses a sum of losses, so duplicating the training
set changes the relative strength of the penalty even at fixed \(C\).

>> Deriving the dual and the support vectors

Introduce multipliers \(\alpha_i\geq0\) for the margin constraints and
\(\mu_i\geq0\) for the non-negative slacks. The Lagrangian is

$$
L=\tfrac12\lVert w\rVert^2+C\sum_i\xi_i+
\sum_i\alpha_i(1-\xi_i-y_i(w^\top x_i+b))-\sum_i\mu_i\xi_i.
$$
\alttext(The Lagrangian is the primal objective plus alpha times each margin constraint violation, minus mu times each slack.)

Stationarity gives \(w=\sum_i\alpha_i y_i x_i\), \(\sum_i\alpha_i y_i=0\)
and \(C-\alpha_i-\mu_i=0\). Eliminating \(w,b,\xi\) leaves

$$
D=\max_\alpha\left\{\sum_i\alpha_i-\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}\alpha_i\alpha_jy_iy_jK(x_i,x_j)\right\},
\qquad 0\leq\alpha_i\leq C,\quad \sum_i\alpha_iy_i=0.
$$
\alttext(Maximise the sum of alpha minus one half the kernel quadratic form weighted by the signed alphas, with each alpha between zero and C and the signed sum equal to zero.)

For the linear model \(K(x,z)=x^\top z\). Complementary slackness gives the KKT rules:

$$
\alpha_i=0\Rightarrow y_if_i\geq1,\qquad
0<\alpha_i<C\Rightarrow y_if_i=1,\qquad
\alpha_i=C\Rightarrow y_if_i\leq1.
$$
\alttext(A zero coefficient requires a signed decision at least one; a coefficient strictly inside its box requires exactly one; a coefficient at C requires at most one.)

Only non-zero coefficients contribute to
\(f(x)=\sum_i\alpha_i y_iK(x_i,x)+b\). These observations are the support vectors.
An observation exactly on the margin need not have a positive coefficient when the solution
is degenerate. `training_details()` returns all pair rows, including zeros;
`num_support_vectors()` counts distinct observations across pairs with coefficients above
\(10^{-8}\), the threshold used for prediction. The current implementation retains the
training design as well, so sparse predictions do not imply sparse training storage.

For positive-semidefinite kernels the primal is convex and admits strictly feasible slacks,
so strong duality holds. Any feasible primal and dual iterates obey \(P\geq P^*\geq D\).
Consequently the gap \(P-D\) bounds objective suboptimality, provided the equality and box
constraints are also satisfied numerically. `diagnostics()` reports the gap, the absolute
equality residual and the largest violation of the KKT rules. For that residual it treats
coefficients within \(\min(10^{-8},10^{-6}C)\) of a box face as on the face, avoiding
misleading residuals from round-off coefficients. It does not clip a negative gap.

>> Kernels: a linear separator in another space

If \(K(x,z)=\phi(x)^\top\phi(z)\), the dual and decision need only the kernel;
the feature map never has to be materialised. The available kernels are

$$
\begin{aligned}
K_{\rm lin}(x,z)&=x^\top z, &
K_{\rm poly}(x,z)&=(\gamma x^\top z+r)^d,\\
K_{\rm RBF}(x,z)&=\exp(-\gamma\lVert x-z\rVert^2), &
K_{\rm sig}(x,z)&=\tanh(\gamma x^\top z+r).
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(The linear kernel is a dot product. The polynomial kernel raises gamma times the dot product plus r to integer degree d. The radial kernel exponentiates minus gamma times squared distance. The sigmoid kernel is the hyperbolic tangent of gamma times the dot product plus r.)

Linear and RBF kernels are positive semidefinite; so is the polynomial kernel for positive
\(\gamma\), non-negative \(r\) and positive integer \(d\). Arbitrary sigmoid parameters do
not have that guarantee, so its gap is not generally an optimality certificate. We keep the
sigmoid option in the iris comparison to show that sharing the same interface does not
make every kernel choice a convex SVM.

The defaults are RBF, \(C=1\), \(\gamma=1/p\), \(r=0\), \(d=3\).
`gamma <= 0` requests the automatic positive value; it does not fit a negative-gamma RBF.
Numeric design columns are standardised by the training mean and sample standard deviation
(divisor \(n-1\)); near-constant columns use scale 1. Scaling is disabled in the hand examples
so all coordinates and margins are in their stated units. Automatic gamma is based on the
number of design columns, not on the variance as in some other libraries' defaults.

>> Two-coordinate SMO updates and implementation limits

Changing just one \(\alpha_i\) would break \(\sum_i\alpha_i y_i=0\).
SMO changes two. With \(E_i=f_i-y_i\) and
\(\eta=K_{ii}+K_{jj}-2K_{ij}>0\), the unconstrained step and equality-preserving partner are

$$
\alpha_j' = \operatorname{clip}_{[L,H]}
\left(\alpha_j+\frac{y_j(E_i-E_j)}{\eta}\right),\qquad
\alpha_i'=\alpha_i+y_iy_j(\alpha_j-\alpha_j').
$$
\alttext(Update alpha j by y j times the difference of errors divided by eta, clipped to its feasible interval; adjust alpha i by y i y j times the old minus new alpha j.)

The interval comes from intersecting both boxes with the equality line:

$$
[L,H]=\begin{cases}
[\max(0,\alpha_j-\alpha_i),\min(C,C+\alpha_j-\alpha_i)],&y_i\ne y_j,\\
[\max(0,\alpha_i+\alpha_j-C),\min(C,\alpha_i+\alpha_j)],&y_i=y_j.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(For opposite labels, the lower bound is the maximum of zero and alpha j minus alpha i, and the upper bound is the minimum of C and C plus alpha j minus alpha i. For equal labels, the bounds use the sum of the two coefficients.)

For two points \(-a,+a\), labels \(+1,-1\), starting at zero with a linear kernel,
\(E_i=-1,E_j=+1,\eta=4a^2\); the first step is already the exact solution
\(\alpha_i=\alpha_j=\min(C,1/(2a^2))\). The worked cost path below checks this.

The library uses simplified SMO with deterministic random partner selection, not Platt's
full working-set heuristics. It skips non-positive-curvature pairs and changes below
\(10^{-7}\). Fitting stops after `max_passes` unchanged sweeps, or after
\(\max(200,20\,\texttt{max_passes})\) total sweeps. Either can occur before all KKT
conditions meet `tolerance`; residuals must be inspected. Identical mapped observations,
indefinite kernels and large cost values deserve particular care.

For a binary problem with \(m\) rows and \(p\) predictors, the stored Gram matrix costs
\(O(m^2)\) space and \(O(m^2p)\) construction time. Each sweep costs \(O(m^2)\)
since decisions are recomputed from all coefficients; there is no error cache. This is a
small-data implementation. Prediction costs \(O(sp)\) per pair with \(s\) support vectors.
For \(k\) classes there are \(k(k-1)/2\) models; they vote for the final class, with ties
resolved in sorted class order. Votes are counts, not posterior probabilities.

> Simple examples

>> Four points: which observations hold up the margin?

Take points \((-2,0),(-1,0)\) in class `left` and \((1,0),(2,0)\) in class `right`.
The largest-margin boundary is \(x=0\), with \(w=(-1,0)\), \(b=0\).
Only the inner two points need coefficients: \(\alpha=(0,1/2,1/2,0)\).
Thus \(\lVert w\rVert^2=1\), full margin width 2 and primal and dual objectives both
\(1/2\). Moving either outer point farther away leaves that optimum unchanged.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/svm_margin.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data;
  data.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{-2,-1,1,2});
  data.add_column("z", std::vector<double>{0,0,0,0});
  data.add_column("label", std::vector<std::string>{"left","left","right","right"});
  dm::stats::SVMOptions opt;
  opt.kernel=dm::stats::SVMKernel::Linear; opt.cost=10; opt.scale=false;
  const dm::stats::SVM model(data,"label ~ x + z",opt);
  auto plot=model.plot_decision_regions("x","z",100,std::array<double,4>{-3,3,-1,1.8});
  plot.segments({-1,1},{-1,-1},{-1,1},{1.8,1.8},{90,90,90},1.2)
      .segments({0},{-1},{0},{1.8},{0,0,0},1.5)
      .title("Boundary x = 0; margin planes x = -1 and +1").size(800,430);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.save_svg("build/svm_margin.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",model.training_details().select({"row","label","alpha","decision","margin","slack"}).to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| row | label | alpha | decision | margin | slack |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | left | 0.0 | 2 | 2 | 0 |
| 1 | left | 0.5 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 2 | right | 0.5 | -1 | 1 | 0 |
| 3 | right | 0.0 | -2 | 2 | 0 |

\image(figures/svm_margin.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Four collinear points: the two inner coefficients are one half, the outer ones zero. Shading is the predicted class; the grey lines are the margin planes.)
\alttext(Four points lie on the horizontal axis, two blue to the left and two red to the right. A black vertical boundary is at zero; grey vertical margin lines at minus one and plus one pass through the inner observations.)

>> Two points: a soft margin need not cause a classification error

With just \((-1,+1)\) and \((+1,-1)\) in one predictor, the equality constraint requires
both coefficients to equal \(t\). The dual is \(D(t)=2t-2t^2\), whose unconstrained
maximum is \(t=1/2\). At cost \(C=0.1\), clipping gives \(t=0.1\),
\(w=-0.2,b=0\). Both signed decisions are 0.2 and both slacks 0.8.
The primal is \(0.02+0.1(1.6)=0.18\), exactly the dual \(0.2-0.02\).
Both points are still classified correctly. The formal planes \(f=\pm1\) now lie at
\(x=\mp5\): they enclose the training points rather than touch them.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/svm_soft_margin.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data;
  data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-1,1});
  data.add_column("label",std::vector<std::string>{"left","right"});
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  std::vector<double> costs, alphas, margins, slacks, primal, dual;
  for (double cost : {0.1,0.5,10.0}) {
    dm::stats::SVMOptions opt;
    opt.kernel=dm::stats::SVMKernel::Linear; opt.scale=false; opt.cost=cost;
    const dm::stats::SVM model(data,"label ~ x",opt);
    const auto rows=model.training_details(), diag=model.diagnostics();
    costs.push_back(cost); alphas.push_back(rows.double_at("alpha",0));
    margins.push_back(rows.double_at("margin",0)); slacks.push_back(rows.double_at("slack",0));
    primal.push_back(diag.double_at("primal",0)); dual.push_back(diag.double_at("dual",0));
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame query; query.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-6,6});
    const auto f=model.decision_function(query);
    plot.lines({-6,6},{f(0,0),f(1,0)},std::format("C = {}",cost),
        cost==0.1 ? dm::RGB{37,99,235} : cost==0.5 ? dm::RGB{220,38,38} : dm::RGB{22,163,74});
  }
  plot.abline_h(1,{100,100,100}).abline_h(-1,{100,100,100})
      .title("Two-point decision functions").x_label("x").y_label("f(x), positive means left")
      .x_limits(-6,6).y_limits(-7,10).size(800,470);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build"); plot.save_svg("build/svm_soft_margin.svg");
  table.add_column("C",costs); table.add_column("alpha",alphas); table.add_column("margin",margins);
  table.add_column("slack",slacks); table.add_column("primal",primal); table.add_column("dual",dual);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| C | alpha | margin | slack | primal | dual |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.8 | 0.18 | 0.18 |
| 0.5 | 0.5 | 1.0 | 0.0 | 0.50 | 0.50 |
| 10.0 | 0.5 | 1.0 | 0.0 | 0.50 | 0.50 |

\image(figures/svm_soft_margin.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The cost clips the coefficient at C until C reaches one half. The C=0.5 and C=10 decision lines coincide.)
\alttext(A shallow blue descending line for cost 0.1 crosses decision levels plus and minus one at x minus and plus five. The steeper red and green coincident lines for costs 0.5 and 10 cross those levels at minus and plus one. All cross zero at the origin.)

>> Four corners: why XOR needs a feature map

Put class `same` at \((-1,-1),(1,1)\), and class `unlike` at
\((-1,1),(1,-1)\). A linear separator cannot classify all four: the midpoint of
each pair is the origin, which would have to lie strictly on both sides.
For the degree-two kernel \(K(x,z)=(x^\top z)^2\), an explicit map is
\(\phi(x)=(x_1^2,\sqrt2x_1x_2,x_2^2)\). The coordinate \(x_1x_2\) alone
separates them. A maximum-margin solution is \(f(x)=x_1x_2\), with
\(\lVert w_\phi\rVert^2=1/2\) and objective \(1/4\).

For RBF with \(\gamma=1\), symmetry permits equal coefficients. Each signed decision is
\(t(1+e^{-8}-2e^{-4})\). Set it to one to obtain

$$
t=\frac1{(1-e^{-4})^2},\qquad \lVert w_\phi\rVert^2=4t,\qquad P=D=2t.
$$
\alttext(The common RBF coefficient is one divided by the square of one minus e to the minus four. The squared normal norm is four times this coefficient, and both objectives are twice it.)

The polynomial map sends opposite corners to identical features, so its individual
coefficients are non-unique; only their sum within each class is fixed at \(1/4\).
SMO can distribute that sum unevenly. This is why the table checks the norm and objective,
not an invented unique coefficient of \(1/8\) at every corner.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/svm_xor.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data, table;
  data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-1,1,-1,1});
  data.add_column("z",std::vector<double>{-1,1,1,-1});
  data.add_column("label",std::vector<std::string>{"same","same","unlike","unlike"});
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  std::vector<std::string> names; std::vector<double> errors,norms,duals,gaps;
  for (auto kernel : {dm::stats::SVMKernel::Polynomial,dm::stats::SVMKernel::Radial}) {
    dm::stats::SVMOptions opt;
    opt.kernel=kernel; opt.degree=2; opt.gamma=1; opt.cost=10; opt.scale=false; opt.tolerance=1e-7;
    const dm::stats::SVM model(data,"label ~ x + z",opt);
    const auto diag=model.diagnostics(); names.push_back(std::format("{}",kernel));
    errors.push_back(model.error(data)); norms.push_back(diag.double_at("norm_squared",0));
    duals.push_back(diag.double_at("dual",0)); gaps.push_back(diag.double_at("gap",0));
    layout.add(model.plot_decision_regions("x","z",120,std::array<double,4>{-2,2,-2,3.2}).title(names.back()));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,480).fit_panels().save_svg("build/svm_xor.svg");
  table.add_column("kernel",names); table.add_column("error",errors); table.add_column("norm_squared",norms);
  table.add_column("dual",duals); table.add_column("gap",gaps);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| kernel | error | norm_squared | dual | gap |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Polynomial | 0 | 0.500000 | 0.250000 | 0.000000 |
| Radial | 0 | 4.150651 | 2.075326 | 0.000001 |

\image(figures/svm_xor.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two kernels separate four XOR corners. Norms are measured in different feature spaces, so their magnitudes do not rank the models.)
\alttext(Two panels show blue points at the bottom-left and top-right corners, red points at the other two corners. Pale regions follow the corner labels. Both polynomial and radial kernels produce alternating class regions meeting near the coordinate axes.)

> Checking the theory

>> A closed form over scales and costs

For points \(-a,+a\) the exact solution is

$$
\alpha^*=\min(C,1/(2a^2)),\quad w=-2a\alpha^*,\quad b=0,\quad
\lVert w\rVert^2=4a^2(\alpha^*)^2,\quad y_if_i=2a^2\alpha^*.
$$
\alttext(The optimal coefficient is the minimum of C and one over twice a squared. The weight is minus twice a times that coefficient, the bias is zero, the squared norm is four a squared times the squared coefficient, and each signed decision is two a squared times the coefficient.)

The cost at which the hard-margin solution becomes feasible is \(C=1/(2a^2)\).
Scaling the coordinates by \(a\) moves this transition quadratically. We check four
scales and 25 logarithmically spaced costs for each scale. Markers are fitted
coefficients; lines are the analytic formula. This is a solver check, not a test of
statistical generalisation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/svm_cost_path.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> scales,alpha_error,gap_error,kkt;
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74},{147,51,234}};
  for (double a : {0.5,1.0,2.0,4.0}) {
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame data;
    data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-a,a});
    data.add_column("label",std::vector<std::string>{"left","right"});
    std::vector<double> x,expected,observed;
    double ae=0,ge=0,ke=0;
    for (int i=0;i<=24;++i) {
      const double logc=-2+i/8.0, c=std::pow(10.0,logc);
      dm::stats::SVMOptions opt;
      opt.kernel=dm::stats::SVMKernel::Linear; opt.cost=c; opt.scale=false;
      const dm::stats::SVM model(data,"label ~ x",opt);
      const auto diag=model.diagnostics();
      const double alpha=model.training_details().double_at("alpha",0), exact=std::min(c,1/(2*a*a));
      x.push_back(logc); expected.push_back(exact); observed.push_back(alpha);
      ae=std::max(ae,std::abs(alpha-exact)); ge=std::max(ge,std::abs(diag.double_at("gap",0)));
      ke=std::max(ke,diag.double_at("max_kkt_violation",0));
    }
    const auto colour=colours[scales.size()];
    plot.lines(x,expected,std::format("a = {}",a),colour).points(x,observed,"",colour,2.5);
    scales.push_back(a); alpha_error.push_back(ae); gap_error.push_back(ge); kkt.push_back(ke);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.title("Exact coefficient paths: lines and fitted markers").x_label("log10(C)").y_label("alpha")
      .y_limits(0,3.1).size(850,490).save_svg("build/svm_cost_path.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("a",scales); table.add_column("max_alpha_error",alpha_error);
  table.add_column("max_abs_gap",gap_error); table.add_column("max_KKT",kkt);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(12));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| a | max_alpha_error | max_abs_gap | max_KKT |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.5 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1.0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 2.0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 4.0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |

\image(figures/svm_cost_path.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A hundred fits over four scales and 25 costs each. The largest coefficient error, absolute gap and KKT residual per scale are shown below the paths.)
\alttext(Four rising coefficient curves flatten at 2, 0.5, 0.125 and 0.03125 for scales 0.5, 1, 2 and 4. Fitted markers lie on the analytic lines; larger scales reach their plateau at lower costs.)

> A dataset example: iris

>> The data and an evaluation protocol

`dm::datasets::iris()` supplies 150 flowers, 50 of each species, with four measurements
in centimetres. Unlike the artificial examples, all predictors are now standardised.
The summary is descriptive; model means and scales are estimated only from training rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  std::print("{} flowers, {} columns.\n\n",iris.nrows(),iris.ncols());
  std::print("{}\n",iris | dm::group_by("Species")
      | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Sepal.Length","Sepal length"),
                      dm::agg::mean("Sepal.Width","Sepal width"),
                      dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","Petal length"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","Petal width"))
      | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
150 flowers, 5 columns.

| Species | n | Sepal length | Sepal width | Petal length | Petal width |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 5.006 | 3.428 | 1.462 | 0.246 |
| versicolor | 50 | 5.936 | 2.770 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 50 | 6.588 | 2.974 | 5.552 | 2.026 |
// result_end
\caption(Species sizes and mean measurements in the built-in iris dataset.)

We predeclare four \(C=1\) kernel models and compare them on the same stratified
70%/30% split (seed 42), using all four measurements. These are descriptive comparisons;
we do not choose a winner and reuse this holdout as an unbiased estimate of that selection.
No hyperparameter search is performed. A 45-flower holdout has resolution only \(1/45\),
so a difference of one flower is weak evidence about generalisation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.3,42,"Species");
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<double> train_error,test_error,support,max_gap,max_kkt;
  for (auto kernel : {dm::stats::SVMKernel::Linear,dm::stats::SVMKernel::Polynomial,
                      dm::stats::SVMKernel::Radial,dm::stats::SVMKernel::Sigmoid}) {
    dm::stats::SVMOptions opt; opt.kernel=kernel;
    const dm::stats::SVM model(train,"Species ~ .",opt);
    names.push_back(std::format("{}",kernel)); train_error.push_back(model.error(train));
    test_error.push_back(model.error(test)); support.push_back(model.num_support_vectors());
    const auto diag=model.diagnostics();
    const auto gaps=diag.numeric_column("gap").values(), kkts=diag.numeric_column("max_kkt_violation").values();
    max_gap.push_back(*std::max_element(gaps.begin(),gaps.end()));
    max_kkt.push_back(*std::max_element(kkts.begin(),kkts.end()));
  }
  std::print("Training: {}, holdout: {}.\n\n",train.nrows(),test.nrows());
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("kernel",names); table.add_column("train_error",train_error); table.add_column("holdout_error",test_error);
  table.add_column("distinct_support",support); table.add_column("max_pair_gap",max_gap); table.add_column("max_pair_KKT",max_kkt);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(5));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Training: 105, holdout: 45.

| kernel | train_error | holdout_error | distinct_support | max_pair_gap | max_pair_KKT |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Linear | 0.01905 | 0.04444 | 23 | 0.00716 | 0.01894 |
| Polynomial | 0.08571 | 0.13333 | 43 | 0.00093 | 0.00713 |
| Radial | 0.01905 | 0.02222 | 46 | 0.00256 | 0.00096 |
| Sigmoid | 0.10476 | 0.04444 | 47 | 0.00131 | 0.02402 |
// result_end
\caption(Fixed kernel comparisons on the same held-out rows. The sigmoid residuals are not a convex optimality certificate.)

The default RBF model makes one error among the 45 held-out flowers (97.8% accuracy);
linear makes two, polynomial six, and sigmoid two. As an independent check, scikit-learn
1.8.0 `SVC` was fitted on the identical exported training rows after sample-SD scaling,
with explicit gamma 0.25, cost 1, degree 3 and offset 0. Its predictions agree for every
training and held-out row for all four kernels. NumPy reconstruction from the fitted
coefficients independently reproduces the pairwise norm, primal, dual and gap. Agreement
in classification does not imply identical coefficients or exact numerical optimality.

The next table exposes the three binary fits of the default RBF model, at \(\gamma=1/4\).
Support counts here are per pair; their sum can exceed the number of distinct support
observations because a flower participates in two binary problems. The equality residual
checks feasibility separately from the primal–dual gap. The residuals quantify numerical
precision; classification accuracy alone cannot establish an optimal fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.3,42,"Species");
  const dm::stats::SVM model(train,"Species ~ .");
  std::print("{}\n",model.diagnostics().select({"positive_class","negative_class","support_vectors",
      "primal","dual","gap","equality_residual","max_kkt_violation","sweeps"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| positive_class | negative_class | support_vectors | primal | dual | gap | equality_residual | max_kkt_violation | sweeps |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | versicolor | 11 | 3.535635 | 3.533074 | 0.002561 | 0 | 0.000964 | 218 |
| setosa | virginica | 12 | 2.846013 | 2.843887 | 0.002126 | 0 | 0.000928 | 219 |
| versicolor | virginica | 33 | 19.365705 | 19.364182 | 0.001523 | 0 | 0.000798 | 224 |
// result_end
\caption(Per-pair RBF objectives, equality and KKT residuals, and actual sweep counts, including unchanged sweeps.)

>> A visible boundary uses a different, two-predictor model

For visualisation we fit only petal length and width on the same training split.
These figures therefore show different models from the four-predictor table, with automatic
\(\gamma=1/2\). We compare two prechosen costs to illustrate flexibility; no holdout-driven
choice is made. Points are training flowers, colours are observed species and shading is
the predicted class at cell centres. The plotted region is an approximation on a 140 by 140
grid, not an exact algebraic contour.

```{cpp, file="figures/svm_iris_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.3,42,"Species");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for (double c : {0.1,10.0}) {
    dm::stats::SVMOptions opt; opt.cost=c;
    const dm::stats::SVM model(train,"Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width",opt);
    layout.add(model.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length","Petal.Width",140,
        std::array<double,4>{0.5,7.5,-0.1,3.8}).title(std::format("RBF, C = {}",c)));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/svm_iris_regions.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/svm_iris_regions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Petal-only RBF models on the 105 training flowers. The upper part of each plotting box leaves room for the species legend.)
\alttext(Two panels show petal length against petal width. Setosa forms a compact blue cluster at low values; versicolor and virginica occupy the middle and upper-right. Their pale decision regions change between cost 0.1 and cost 10, particularly near the overlapping species.)

> Conclusion

The hand examples connect each coefficient to the geometry: zero coefficients at the outer
points, non-zero support coefficients at the margin, and saturated coefficients inside it.
The two-point solution shows exactly how changing units shifts the cost threshold. XOR
makes the kernel construction tangible, and also exposes non-unique coefficients in a
degenerate feature map. The computed objectives and residuals let us check those statements
without treating the optimiser as a black box.

Iris extends the same binary rule through three pairwise votes. Its training accuracy is a
fit diagnostic; the held-out errors measure performance on different observations. The small
holdout does not establish a universal kernel ranking, nor does a cost sweep by itself prove
overfitting. Scaling, kernel parameters and the numerical stopping rule remain part of the
model specification. This dense-Gram, simplified-SMO implementation suits small datasets;
its residuals and memory cost matter before using it on larger problems.

> References

- C. Cortes and V. Vapnik (1995). *Support-vector networks*. Machine Learning 20, 273–297.
  [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00994018]. Soft margins and the dual formulation.
- J. C. Platt (1998). *Sequential Minimal Optimization: A Fast Algorithm for Training Support Vector Machines*.
  Microsoft Research, MSR-TR-98-14.
  [Technical report|https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/sequential-minimal-optimization-a-fast-algorithm-for-training-support-vector-machines/].
  The two-coordinate optimisation method; the library uses the simplified variant described above.
